~D. Author A argues that no one should travel by rail.#The passage presents rail as beneficial. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when public rail lines are expanding into rural areas. Author A: The rail line will carry more than passengers. It will carry medicine, books, and letters to towns that have waited too long for connection. A community should not fear a road simply because it is made of iron. Author B: A rail line may bring letters, but it also brings buyers who see land only as numbers on paper. Before we cheer the engine, we should ask who owns the tracks and who must move when the price of progress rises. How does Author B reason about the rail line? {
=A. Author B warns that rail expansion may bring displacement and outside control.#Correct. Author B asks who owns the tracks and who must move. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Author B celebrates the rail line as pure progress.#Author B is cautious, not celebratory. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Author B focuses only on the beauty of engines.#The engine is mentioned as part of a warning about progress. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Author B says letters should never be delivered.#Author B acknowledges letters but says that benefit is not the whole issue. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

